<Project>

  <!--
    Keeps a game's authoring-schema FILE in step with its [Authored] records.

    The schema generator compiles the document into the game assembly as a constant; the editors
    (the Godot addon, the Blender addon, the browser editor) read it as a file, at authoring time,
    before anything is built. Left to a hand-run command that file rots silently: the game keeps
    working — it never reads it — while the editor's Add Component picker offers components that
    no longer exist, or omits ones that do.

    Opt in from the project whose assembly CARRIES the schema (the game core):

        <ParadiseAuthoringSchemaPath>$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/../data/authoring-schema.json</ParadiseAuthoringSchemaPath>

    The dump reads the constant out of the built assembly's metadata (tools/ in this package), so
    nothing is loaded or executed and it costs a file copy. Inputs/Outputs keep it to builds where
    the assembly actually changed.

    THE INPUT IS $(TargetPath), NOT @(Compile), and the difference is load-bearing once
    ParadiseAuthoringScanReferences is in play. @(Compile) is the compiling project's OWN sources,
    which is the whole story only while the project that declares the components is also the one
    that dumps them. A launcher that dumps the merged schema of everything it references declares
    none of them: adding a component to a referenced library changed no @(Compile) item, the target
    was skipped as up to date, and the file silently kept describing yesterday's game — the exact
    drift the dump exists to prevent, reintroduced by its own incrementality.

    $(TargetPath) is the honest input because it is what the tool READS. MSBuild rebuilds it
    whenever any reference changes, so it moves exactly when the schema can have moved.

    Deliberately skipped under ContinuousIntegrationBuild: a game's drift test asserts the
    committed file matches what the generator emits, and CI regenerating the file first would make
    that test compare it against itself and pass regardless. Locally this target keeps you honest;
    in CI the test does.
  -->

  <!--
    Reference scanning, off by default.

    A project that declares [Authored] types publishes a schema for its OWN. Set this on a project
    that declares none but references several — a launcher over a game split into Core/Game/Ui —
    and its schema becomes the union: every referenced assembly's published document merged into
    one, with the local declarations winning any id collision. That is what lets the schema FILE
    be dumped from the one assembly that sees the whole game, rather than from whichever library
    happens to hold the components.

      <ParadiseAuthoringScanReferences>true</ParadiseAuthoringScanReferences>

    The merge takes each reference's already-generated constant verbatim, so a merged component is
    byte-identical to what that assembly would have dumped alone — including field defaults, which
    are property initializers and exist only in source. Re-deriving them from metadata would drop
    every one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="ParadiseAuthoringScanReferences" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ParadiseAuthoringSchemaDumpTool Condition="'$(ParadiseAuthoringSchemaDumpTool)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../tools/net10.0/Paradise.Authoring.SchemaDump.dll</ParadiseAuthoringSchemaDumpTool>
    <!-- The dotnet muxer BY FULL PATH, never bare "dotnet": builds launched from a GUI process
         (the Blender addon's rebuild-on-change watcher) run with a PATH that has no dotnet on
         it, and a bare invocation dies with exit code 127 while the build that spawned this
         very target was itself run by a perfectly good dotnet. DOTNET_HOST_PATH is that host,
         set by the CLI for every build it runs. -->
    <_ParadiseDotNetHost Condition="'$(DOTNET_HOST_PATH)' != ''">$(DOTNET_HOST_PATH)</_ParadiseDotNetHost>
    <_ParadiseDotNetHost Condition="'$(_ParadiseDotNetHost)' == ''">dotnet</_ParadiseDotNetHost>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="ParadiseDumpAuthoringSchema"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(ParadiseAuthoringSchemaPath)' != '' and '$(ContinuousIntegrationBuild)' != 'true'"
          Inputs="$(TargetPath);$(MSBuildAllProjects)"
          Outputs="$(ParadiseAuthoringSchemaPath)">
    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="Paradise: re-dumping the authoring schema (the assembly changed)." />
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(_ParadiseDotNetHost)&quot; exec &quot;$(ParadiseAuthoringSchemaDumpTool)&quot; &quot;$(TargetPath)&quot; &quot;$(ParadiseAuthoringSchemaPath)&quot;" />
  </Target>

</Project>
